### Host Discovery

Goal: identify which systems are online before deep scanning.
Best practice: store every scan for comparison, documentation, reporting.
Most effective method: ICMP echo request.

**Scan entire network range:**
```bash
sudo nmap 10.129.2.0/24 -sn -oA tnet | grep for | cut -d" " -f5
```
| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| 10.129.2.0/24 | Target network range |
| -sn | Disables port scanning |
| -oA tnet | Stores results in all formats with name 'tnet' |

---

### Scan IP List

**View predefined list:**
```bash
cat hosts.lst
```

**Scan from list:**
```bash
sudo nmap -sn -oA tnet -iL hosts.lst | grep for | cut -d" " -f5
```
| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| -iL | Performs scan against targets in provided list |

> Note: If only 3 of 7 hosts appear active, remaining hosts likely ignore ICMP echo requests due to firewall configuration.

---

### Scan Multiple IPs

**Specific IPs:**
```bash
sudo nmap -sn -oA tnet 10.129.2.18 10.129.2.19 10.129.2.20 | grep for | cut -d" " -f5
```

**Consecutive range:**
```bash
sudo nmap -sn -oA tnet 10.129.2.18-20 | grep for | cut -d" " -f5
```

---

### Scan Single IP

Determine if host is alive before scanning ports and services.

**Basic single host scan:**
```bash
sudo nmap 10.129.2.18 -sn -oA host
```

**Force ICMP echo requests (instead of ARP):**
```bash
sudo nmap 10.129.2.18 -sn -oA host -PE --packet-trace
```
| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| -PE | Performs ping scan using ICMP echo requests |
| --packet-trace | Shows all packets sent and received |

**Show reason host marked alive:**
```bash
sudo nmap 10.129.2.18 -sn -oA host -PE --reason
```
| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| --reason | Displays reason for specific result |

**Disable ARP, use ICMP only:**
```bash
sudo nmap 10.129.2.18 -sn -oA host -PE --packet-trace --disable-arp-ping
```

